; P13 M0 -- what a Battlezone frame costs, before any game code exists.
;
;   erall                        <-- REQUIRED, see below
;   load "p13m0
;   p13m0
;
; This is the gate in docs/battlezone-design.md section 16.  The design's whole
; budget is host measurements scaled by 180x (section 3), and 180 was calibrated
; against P11 M0's board figures rather than measured on a board directly.  Every
; number below therefore has a prediction attached to it, and the run is worth
; taking only because some of them will be wrong.
;
; FIVE QUESTIONS, in the order the design asks them:
;
;   Q1  What does a LONG line cost?  Section 10 assumes a 200-pixel edge costs
;       about what P11 measured for a 17-pixel `fd` -- 60 us -- because
;       `screen_gfx_line` marks its dirty region once from an accumulated
;       bounding box rather than per pixel.  That is inference from the source,
;       not a reading, and it is the largest unmeasured number in the design:
;       55 edges at 60 us is 3.3 ms and at 150 us it is 8.3.
;   Q2  What does the SPLIT-MODE present cost?  Section 6 reads `limit` in
;       `screen_gfx_blit_dirty` and predicts 240 rows for 19.7 ms against P11's
;       measured 26.3 full-screen.  What is not certain is whether the 80 rows
;       `clean` marks and the present never sends cost anything.
;   Q3  Does the 180x ratio hold on THIS board?  If it does not, every estimate
;       in the design moves together and section 12 is re-derived rather than
;       re-argued.  The two calibration loops are P11 M0's, unchanged, so the
;       numbers are directly comparable with 42-44.5 us and 4.5-7 us.
;   Q4  What does one box cost to project?  Section 8.1's ground-column trick,
;       predicted at 2.16 ms.
;   Q5  What does the column-form enemy cost?  Section 8.2's 6.0 ms is the
;       estimate the design is least confident in, because unlike the box figure
;       nothing has ever measured one.
;
; Then a FRAME SERIES at 1, 2, 4 and 8 visible objects, with the projection pass
; read apart from the drawing pass and the present read apart from both -- which
; is the split P9 M5 wished it had and P11 M1 built.
;
; FOUR THINGS THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO, each taken from P11 M0's list
; because each was learned the hard way there:
;
;   * It presents with `refresh`, not `sync`.  The number wanted is the work,
;     not the cadence; `sync` would wait out the rest of the frame and report
;     the wait.
;   * It clears with `clean`, not `cs`.  `cs` restores automatic refresh and
;     would silently end the measurement.
;   * It runs in `splitscreen` ON PURPOSE and says so in the report.  In text
;     mode `screen_gfx_blit_dirty` returns immediately, so the present column
;     reads zero and the frame figure is a Logo body with no present in it --
;     the correction P9 had to make to its entire first series of numbers.
;     Q2 measures `fullscreen` as its control and puts them side by side.
;   * It holds the scene still.  The camera does not move between frames, so
;     the dirty region is the same one every time and the present figure is not
;     quietly averaging two different scenes.
;
; ONE THING IT OVER-MEASURES, and it is worth knowing which way: every object in
; the series is a full 12-edge box drawn whole.  A real frame culls some of them
; laterally -- `window` skips out-of-bounds pixels per pixel, so an object half
; off the screen costs its loop iterations and not its stores.  So the drawing
; column is an upper bound, and the pessimism is deliberate.
;
; `erall` is not optional.  MAX_PROCEDURES is a hard 128 and a workspace still
; holding another program can run out of procedure slots, at which point `load`
; stops with `out of space` -- the procedure table talking, not memory.
;
; The report goes to `p13m0.txt` as well as the screen, because numbers on the
; PicoCalc's display cannot be copied anywhere.  Runs append.

;==========================================================================
; The camera and the viewport
;
; `hz` is 40 and not 0, and that is section 6's consequence rather than a taste:
; split mode presents pixel rows 0-239, pixel row is `-y + 160`, so the visible
; band is turtle y in [-79, 160] and its optical centre is +40.  Every screen
; constant here is cut against that.
;
; `k` 260 puts the half-width 160 at atan(160/260) = 31.6 degrees, so the
; horizontal field of view is 63 degrees -- close to the cabinet's.

make "k        260     ; focal length in turtle steps
make "eye       12     ; camera height above the plain
make "near       8     ; nearer than this and the object is culled whole
make "far      900     ; further than this and it is not worth drawing
make "hz        40     ; the horizon line, and the viewport's optical centre
make "half      20     ; obstacle half-width
make "boxh      40     ; obstacle height
make "ehalf     14     ; enemy hull half-width
make "ebodyh    16     ; enemy hull height

; WINDOW, and it is load-bearing rather than tidiness. The default is `wrap`,
; which folds a point past the edge back onto the far side: a horizon point at
; x = -170.8 comes out at +149.2 and the segment into it is stroked across the
; whole screen. Design section 9 says so and this file did not do it -- caught
; by `test_no_horizon_segment_spans_the_whole_screen`, not by any timing.
;
; `window` also gives the projection what it needs: a vertex off the side of
; the screen stays where it belongs and `screen_gfx_line` skips its pixels.
window

make "px         0
make "pz         0
make "ph         0
make "cs         1     ; cos :ph, hoisted once a frame
make "sn         0     ; sin :ph
make "a          0     ; :half * :cs, hoisted once a frame for every box
make "b          0     ; :half * :sn

; Eight obstacles.  The series draws the first :shown of them, and they are
; placed so that all eight are in front of a camera at the origin facing 0 --
; a culled object costs four comparisons and no divides, and a series that
; measured culling would not be measuring drawing.
make "ox [-60 70 -30 110]
make "ox se :ox [-140 40 -95 150]
make "oz [160 210 300 330]
make "oz se :oz [420 480 560 640]

; Four ground columns: screen x, and the two screen heights that share the
; divide.  Mutated in place -- a list built per box per frame would allocate.
make "colx [0 0 0 0]
make "colb [0 0 0 0]
make "colt [0 0 0 0]

to cam.setup
  make "cs cos :ph
  make "sn sin :ph
  make "a :half * :cs
  make "b :half * :sn
end

;==========================================================================
; Projection
;
; Section 8.1: an obstacle is axis-aligned and stands on the plain, so its eight
; vertices are four ground columns lifted to two heights.  With `a` and `b`
; hoisted for every box in the world, the four corners are eight ADDS and no
; multiplies, and the top and bottom of a column share one divide.
;
; The four corners come out in order around the box, so the quads chain.
;
; UNROLLED on purpose.  A `repeat` over the four would need `repcount` and an
; `item` walk per corner to find its offsets, and this is the loop the whole
; budget is linear in.  It is also what the game would write.
;
; Outputs `true` if the box is drawable and `false` if any column is inside the
; near plane -- ANY, not all: one corner behind you swings the projection
; through infinity and throws a line across the whole screen (section 9).
to project.box :dx :dz
  local "xc local "zc local "z1 local "z2 local "z3 local "z4 local "iz
  make "xc (:dx * :cs) - (:dz * :sn)
  make "zc (:dz * :cs) + (:dx * :sn)
  make "z1 (:zc + :a) + :b
  make "z2 (:zc - :a) + :b
  make "z3 (:zc - :a) - :b
  make "z4 (:zc + :a) - :b
  if :near > :z1 [output "false]
  if :near > :z2 [output "false]
  if :near > :z3 [output "false]
  if :near > :z4 [output "false]
  make "iz :k / :z1
  .setitem 1 :colx ((:xc + :a) - :b) * :iz
  .setitem 1 :colb :hz - (:eye * :iz)
  .setitem 1 :colt :hz + ((:boxh - :eye) * :iz)
  make "iz :k / :z2
  .setitem 2 :colx ((:xc + :a) + :b) * :iz
  .setitem 2 :colb :hz - (:eye * :iz)
  .setitem 2 :colt :hz + ((:boxh - :eye) * :iz)
  make "iz :k / :z3
  .setitem 3 :colx ((:xc - :a) + :b) * :iz
  .setitem 3 :colb :hz - (:eye * :iz)
  .setitem 3 :colt :hz + ((:boxh - :eye) * :iz)
  make "iz :k / :z4
  .setitem 4 :colx ((:xc - :a) - :b) * :iz
  .setitem 4 :colb :hz - (:eye * :iz)
  .setitem 4 :colt :hz + ((:boxh - :eye) * :iz)
  output "true
end

;==========================================================================
; Drawing
;
; Twelve edges: the bottom quad, the top quad, and the four verticals that join
; them.  Every one is `(setpos x y)` with the pen down -- ONE statement, one
; straight line, nothing allocated.  That spelling is B48's fix (2026-08-23);
; before it the choices were `setpos list :x :y`, which conses two cells a call,
; or `setx`/`sety`, which draws an L.
;
; Twenty statements for twelve edges.  The design's section 10 guessed sixteen,
; and the difference is the four pen-ups the verticals need.
to draw.box
  pu (setpos item 1 :colx item 1 :colb)
  pd (setpos item 2 :colx item 2 :colb)
  (setpos item 3 :colx item 3 :colb)
  (setpos item 4 :colx item 4 :colb)
  (setpos item 1 :colx item 1 :colb)
  pu (setpos item 1 :colx item 1 :colt)
  pd (setpos item 2 :colx item 2 :colt)
  (setpos item 3 :colx item 3 :colt)
  (setpos item 4 :colx item 4 :colt)
  (setpos item 1 :colx item 1 :colt)
  pu (setpos item 1 :colx item 1 :colb)
  pd (setpos item 1 :colx item 1 :colt)
  pu (setpos item 2 :colx item 2 :colb)
  pd (setpos item 2 :colx item 2 :colt)
  pu (setpos item 3 :colx item 3 :colb)
  pd (setpos item 3 :colx item 3 :colt)
  pu (setpos item 4 :colx item 4 :colb)
  pd (setpos item 4 :colx item 4 :colt)
  pu
end

;==========================================================================
; The enemy
;
; Section 8.2, and it is the estimate the design is least sure of.  The hull is
; a box that happens to be rotated, so the column trick still applies -- with
; the enemy's own rotation folded into the camera's, once a frame, rather than
; once a corner.
;
; p and q are the enemy's half-offsets in world space; P and Q are those two
; offsets already camera-transformed.  The transform is linear, so the transform
; of a corner is the centre's transform plus the offset's, and the four corners
; are then plus-and-minus sums exactly as the box's are.  Six statements buy what
; would otherwise be four multiplies a corner.
;
; The gun is two general vertices, because a barrel has no ground column.

make "ex 0   make "ez 300   make "eh 200
make "gunx [0 0]       ; the gun line, projected
make "guny [0 0]

to project.enemy
  local "dx local "dz local "ec local "es local "p local "q
  local "pxc local "pzc local "qxc local "qzc
  local "xc local "zc local "z1 local "z2 local "z3 local "z4 local "iz
  make "dx :ex - :px
  make "dz :ez - :pz
  make "xc (:dx * :cs) - (:dz * :sn)
  make "zc (:dz * :cs) + (:dx * :sn)
  make "ec cos :eh
  make "es sin :eh
  make "p :ehalf * :ec
  make "q :ehalf * :es
  make "pxc (:p * :cs) - (:q * :sn)
  make "pzc (:q * :cs) + (:p * :sn)
  make "qxc (0 - (:q * :cs)) - (:p * :sn)
  make "qzc (:p * :cs) - (:q * :sn)
  make "z1 (:zc + :pzc) + :qzc
  make "z2 (:zc + :pzc) - :qzc
  make "z3 (:zc - :pzc) - :qzc
  make "z4 (:zc - :pzc) + :qzc
  if :near > :z1 [output "false]
  if :near > :z2 [output "false]
  if :near > :z3 [output "false]
  if :near > :z4 [output "false]
  make "iz :k / :z1
  .setitem 1 :colx ((:xc + :pxc) + :qxc) * :iz
  .setitem 1 :colb :hz - (:eye * :iz)
  .setitem 1 :colt :hz + ((:ebodyh - :eye) * :iz)
  make "iz :k / :z2
  .setitem 2 :colx ((:xc + :pxc) - :qxc) * :iz
  .setitem 2 :colb :hz - (:eye * :iz)
  .setitem 2 :colt :hz + ((:ebodyh - :eye) * :iz)
  make "iz :k / :z3
  .setitem 3 :colx ((:xc - :pxc) - :qxc) * :iz
  .setitem 3 :colb :hz - (:eye * :iz)
  .setitem 3 :colt :hz + ((:ebodyh - :eye) * :iz)
  make "iz :k / :z4
  .setitem 4 :colx ((:xc - :pxc) + :qxc) * :iz
  .setitem 4 :colb :hz - (:eye * :iz)
  .setitem 4 :colt :hz + ((:ebodyh - :eye) * :iz)
  ; The gun: two general vertices, from the turret roof out along the facing.
  make "iz :k / :zc
  .setitem 1 :gunx :xc * :iz
  .setitem 1 :guny :hz + ((:ebodyh - :eye) * :iz)
  make "iz :k / ((:zc + :pzc) + :pzc)
  .setitem 2 :gunx ((:xc + :pxc) + :pxc) * :iz
  .setitem 2 :guny :hz + ((:ebodyh - :eye) * :iz)
  output "true
end

to draw.enemy
  draw.box
  pu (setpos item 1 :gunx item 1 :guny)
  pd (setpos item 2 :gunx item 2 :guny)
  pu
end

;==========================================================================
; The horizon
;
; Thirty-two azimuth points, no depth: screen x is (azimuth - heading) scaled,
; and screen y comes straight from the table.  Linear rather than `k * tan`,
; which is what the cabinet's own backdrop amounts to and saves a tangent a
; point.
;
; `mtn.seen` is why this is worth measuring rather than assuming.  The design's
; section 8.4 says "culled to ~12 of 32", and 32 points over 360 degrees at
; 5.06 px a degree are 71 px apart, so a 63-degree field holds about FIVE. If
; that is what the board reports, the design's horizon figure is wrong in the
; cheap direction and the table wants more points, not fewer.

; Built in four pieces because a list literal has to fit on one line -- the
; test harness loads this file a line at a time, as `load` does.
make "mtn [4 9 2 14 7 3 11 5]
make "mtn se :mtn [8 2 16 6 4 10 3 7]
make "mtn se :mtn [12 5 2 9 14 4 8 3]
make "mtn se :mtn [11 6 2 13 5 9 4 7]
make "mtn.step  11.25    ; degrees between points
make "mtn.scale  5.06    ; turtle steps per degree
make "mtn.seen      0    ; how many were actually drawn
make "mtn.runs      0    ; how many separate polylines they formed

; One full scan with a cull test on each point.  The cull is what the design
; proposes to spend, so the run has to price the scan it would replace.
;
; THE RANGE IS SCANNED IN INDEX ORDER AND DRAWN IN SCREEN ORDER, and those are
; not the same thing.  At heading 0 the visible points are indices 1-4 (azimuth
; 0 to 33.75) and 30-32 (326.25 to 348.75, which wrap to -33.75 to -11.25), so
; walking the table start to finish steps from +171 to -171 in one segment and
; strokes a line clear across the screen.  It looks exactly like a rendering
; fault and nothing about the timing would have shown it.
;
; The guard is one comparison a drawn point: a point to the LEFT of the last one
; means the table wrapped, so lift the pen and start a second polyline.  A frame
; has at most two runs, because the visible arc is contiguous in screen space
; and only the table's seam splits it.
to horizon
  local "h local "lastx
  make "mtn.seen 0
  make "mtn.runs 0
  ; 9999 so the FIRST drawn point always takes the pen-up branch. Seeded low,
  ; it joined whatever the pen was last touching.
  make "lastx 9999
  repeat 32 [
    make "h ((repcount - 1) * :mtn.step) - :ph
    if :h > 180 [make "h :h - 360]
    if -180 > :h [make "h :h + 360]
    make "h :h * :mtn.scale
    if 176 > abs :h [
      make "mtn.seen :mtn.seen + 1
      ifelse :h > :lastx [
        (setpos :h :hz + item repcount :mtn)
      ] [
        pu (setpos :h :hz + item repcount :mtn) pd
        make "mtn.runs :mtn.runs + 1
      ]
      make "lastx :h
    ]
  ]
  pu
end

;==========================================================================
; The frame
;
; The projection pass and the drawing pass are separated because they answer
; different questions and have different levers: projection is cut by the object
; cap, drawing by the edge count, and section 13's L4 primitive would collapse
; both at once.  A frame that reported one number could not say which.
;
; `shown` is how many of the eight obstacles this point carries.

make "shown 1
make "proj.ms 0
make "draw.ms 0

; TWO FRAMES, and the pair is the point.
;
; `frame.body` carries the phase timers that split projection from drawing, and
; those timers are not free: four statements an object, up to 36 a frame, which
; at an arithmetic statement's 43 us is about 1.5 ms of the figure they are
; inside.  P11 M4 stated its own 0.15 ms and moved on; at ten times that it is
; worth measuring instead of stating.
;
; `frame.raw` is the same statements in the same order with the timers taken
; out, so it is what the frame actually costs.  The series reports both, and
; the difference IS the instrumentation -- measured, not estimated.
;
; THEY MUST STAY IN STEP.  `test_p13m0.c` pins that both draw the same number of
; lines from the same state, because a split that describes a frame nobody runs
; is worse than no split at all.

to frame.body
  local "t local "i
  cam.setup
  clean
  make "t ticks
  horizon
  make "draw.ms ticks - :t
  make "proj.ms 0
  repeat :shown [
    make "t ticks
    make "i project.box ((item repcount :ox) - :px) ((item repcount :oz) - :pz)
    make "proj.ms :proj.ms + (ticks - :t)
    if :i [
      make "t ticks
      draw.box
      make "draw.ms :draw.ms + (ticks - :t)
    ]
  ]
  make "t ticks
  make "i project.enemy
  make "proj.ms :proj.ms + (ticks - :t)
  if :i [
    make "t ticks
    draw.enemy
    make "draw.ms :draw.ms + (ticks - :t)
  ]
end

to frame.raw
  local "i
  cam.setup
  clean
  horizon
  repeat :shown [
    make "i project.box ((item repcount :ox) - :px) ((item repcount :oz) - :pz)
    if :i [draw.box]
  ]
  make "i project.enemy
  if :i [draw.enemy]
end

;==========================================================================
; The five questions

make "p13m0.frames 200

; THE CLOCK.  Set this to a frequency in MHz and the whole run is taken at it;
; leave it 0 and the board is left wherever it already was.  150 is stock and
; anything above it is an overclock outside the RP2350's datasheet -- see
; `hw.setfrequency` in the reference before using it.
;
; WHY THIS IS WORTH A SWEEP RATHER THAN A NUMBER.  The frame splits into two
; halves that respond to the clock completely differently: the present is the
; SPI wire to the panel and will not move at all, while the body is nothing but
; interpretation and should scale almost exactly.  At three obstacles the split
; is about 19.8 ms that cannot improve and 35 ms that should, so a 2x clock is
; worth about a third of the frame and not half of it.  A sweep says whether
; that is what actually happens; a single reading cannot.
;
; ON A W BOARD THIS ALSO RETUNES THE WIRELESS BUS, and the first version of the
; feature did not.  The processor reaches the wireless chip over a PIO bus
; clocked from the system clock, so at 250 MHz it ran at 62 MHz instead of 37 and
; the chip answered with "hdr mismatch" -- reported from a board within an hour.
; `hw.setfrequency` now takes that bus down, changes the clock and rebuilds it at
; the new rate, so the radio may be up and stays up.
;
; The report carries the frequency it actually ran at, read back from the
; hardware rather than from this variable, because `hw.setfrequency` refuses a
; frequency the PLL cannot make exactly -- and a run that quietly stayed at 150
; would otherwise look like an overclock that bought nothing.
make "p13m0.mhz 0

; Die temperature either side of the run.  The chip gets hotter the faster it
; runs and this is the only thing in the harness that says by how much.
make "p13m0.temp0 0
make "p13m0.temp1 0
make "p13m0.mhz.ran 0

; Q1.  A long edge against a short one, both pen down, both `(setpos x y)`.
; 200 steps is about what a near box's edge spans; 17 is P11 M0's `fd`, so the
; short figure is the one that compares directly with its 60 us.
to time.long.line
  local "t0
  pu (setpos -100 0) pd
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat 500 [(setpos 100 0) (setpos -100 0)]
  pu
  output (ticks - :t0) / 1000
end

to time.short.line
  local "t0
  pu (setpos -8 0) pd
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat 500 [(setpos 9 0) (setpos -8 0)]
  pu
  output (ticks - :t0) / 1000
end

; Q3.  P11 M0's two calibration loops, unchanged, so the numbers compare
; directly with 42-44.5 us and 4.5-7 us.
to time.arith
  local "t0 local "x
  make "x 0
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat 2000 [make "x :x + 1]
  output ((ticks - :t0) * 1000) / 2000
end

to time.bare
  local "t0
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat 2000 []
  output ((ticks - :t0) * 1000) / 2000
end

; Q4 and Q5, on their own and with no drawing in them.
to time.box.proj
  local "t0
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat 200 [ignore project.box 0 300]
  output (ticks - :t0) / 200
end

to time.enemy.proj
  local "t0
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat 200 [ignore project.enemy]
  output (ticks - :t0) / 200
end

to time.box.draw
  local "t0
  ignore project.box 0 300
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat 200 [draw.box]
  output (ticks - :t0) / 200
end

to time.horizon
  local "t0
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat 200 [horizon]
  output (ticks - :t0) / 200
end

; Q2.  The present, measured in both screen modes over the same scene, because
; the saving section 6 predicts is the DIFFERENCE and a single figure cannot
; show it.  `clean` marks the whole canvas in manual mode either way; what
; changes is how many rows the present is willing to send.
to time.present
  local "t0 local "total
  make "total 0
  repeat 20 [
    clean
    horizon
    make "shown 4
    frame.body
    make "t0 ticks
    refresh
    make "total :total + (ticks - :t0)
  ]
  output :total / 20
end

;==========================================================================
; The series

make "p13m0.n    (list 1 2 4 8)
make "p13m0.body (list 0 0 0 0)   ; frame.raw -- what the frame costs
make "p13m0.inst (list 0 0 0 0)   ; frame.body -- the same frame with the timers in
make "p13m0.proj (list 0 0 0 0)
make "p13m0.draw (list 0 0 0 0)
make "p13m0.pres (list 0 0 0 0)
make "p13m0.lo   (list 0 0 0 0)
make "p13m0.hi   (list 0 0 0 0)
make "p13m0.seen (list 0 0 0 0)

; ACCUMULATOR NAMES ARE PREFIXED, and the prefix is load-bearing.
;
; This procedure's body accumulator was called `b` on the first board run, and
; `cam.setup` -- which every frame calls -- does `make "b :half * :sn`. Logo is
; dynamically scoped, so `cam.setup`'s `make` found the CALLER's local and wrote
; the accumulator instead of the global, once a frame, with `sn` at 0. Every
; body figure came back 0.00 ms and the run's most important column was gone.
;
; It is not a shadow in the sense the Asteroids design warns about (one flat
; global namespace, two meanings, one variable). It is worse: the two names are
; in different scopes and the callee still reaches the caller's. `local` protects
; a name from the world; it does not protect the world from a name.
;
; So every accumulator here is `m.something`, and
; `test_the_frame_does_not_write_the_measure_accumulators` walks the list.
to measure :slot
  local "m.b local "m.p local "m.d local "m.r local "m.one local "m.t0
  local "m.lo local "m.hi local "m.ins
  make "shown item :slot :p13m0.n
  make "px 0  make "pz 0  make "ph 0
  setrefresh "manual
  make "m.b 0  make "m.p 0  make "m.d 0  make "m.r 0  make "m.ins 0
  make "m.lo 99999  make "m.hi 0
  repeat :p13m0.frames [
    ; The untimed frame is the one the body, min and max columns report.
    make "m.t0 ticks
    frame.raw
    make "m.one ticks - :m.t0
    make "m.b :m.b + :m.one
    make "m.t0 ticks
    refresh
    make "m.r :m.r + (ticks - :m.t0)
    make "m.one :m.one + (ticks - :m.t0)
    if :m.one < :m.lo [make "m.lo :m.one]
    if :m.one > :m.hi [make "m.hi :m.one]
    ; The instrumented frame runs beside it, outside the figures above, purely
    ; to split projection from drawing.  Its own cost is reported rather than
    ; hidden: `inst` minus `body` is what the phase timers charge.
    make "m.t0 ticks
    frame.body
    make "m.ins :m.ins + (ticks - :m.t0)
    make "m.p :m.p + :proj.ms
    make "m.d :m.d + :draw.ms
  ]
  .setitem :slot :p13m0.body :m.b / :p13m0.frames
  .setitem :slot :p13m0.inst :m.ins / :p13m0.frames
  .setitem :slot :p13m0.proj :m.p / :p13m0.frames
  .setitem :slot :p13m0.draw :m.d / :p13m0.frames
  .setitem :slot :p13m0.pres :m.r / :p13m0.frames
  .setitem :slot :p13m0.lo :m.lo
  .setitem :slot :p13m0.hi :m.hi
  .setitem :slot :p13m0.seen :mtn.seen
end

; Printed twice -- once to the file, once to the screen -- so it is one list of
; lines rather than two that can drift apart.  Punctuation is spelled out in
; words: every mark in a printed list is tokenised as a word of its own, so
; `M1+M2` comes out as `M1 + M2` and `--` as `- -`.
to line.for :slot
  pr (se item :slot :p13m0.n [objects plus the enemy])
  pr (se [  body] item :slot :p13m0.body [ms   present] item :slot :p13m0.pres [ms])
  pr (se [  of the body, projection] item :slot :p13m0.proj
      [ms and drawing] item :slot :p13m0.draw [ms])
  pr (se [  the phase timers cost] (item :slot :p13m0.inst) - (item :slot :p13m0.body)
      [ms and are not in the body figure])
  pr (se [  frame] (item :slot :p13m0.body) + (item :slot :p13m0.pres)
      [ms   min] item :slot :p13m0.lo [ms   max] item :slot :p13m0.hi [ms])
end

; Both of these degrade rather than fail: a board with no sensor, or one that
; cannot retune, still runs the harness and reports 0 for what it could not
; read.  A timing script that dies on its last line has wasted the session.
to read.temp
  local "t
  make "t 0
  catch "error [make "t hw.temperature]
  output :t
end

to read.mhz
  local "f
  make "f 0
  catch "error [make "f hw.frequency]
  output :f
end

; Asked for, not assumed.  If the frequency is refused -- out of range, or one
; the PLL cannot make exactly -- the run continues at whatever the board was
; already doing, and the report says which.
to set.mhz :mhz
  if 0 = :mhz [stop]
  catch "error [hw.setfrequency :mhz]
end

to p13m0.report
  pr [P13 M0 what a Battlezone frame costs, before any game code exists]
  pr (se [frames per point] :p13m0.frames [in splitscreen, present with refresh])
  ifelse 0 = :p13m0.mhz.ran [
    pr [the clock could not be read, so these are at whatever it was]
  ] [
    pr (se [processor at] :p13m0.mhz.ran [MHz, stock is 150])
    if 150 < :p13m0.mhz.ran [pr [that is an overclock, outside the datasheet]]
  ]
  pr (se [die temperature] :p13m0.temp0 [C before,] :p13m0.temp1 [C after])
  pr [the present is the SPI wire, so it should barely move with the clock]
  pr [if it did, read design 12.3 before believing anything else here]
  pr []
  pr [Q3 does the 180x ratio hold]
  pr (se [  arithmetic statement] :arith [us, P11 M0 measured 42 to 44.5])
  pr (se [  bare repeat iteration] :bare [us, P11 M0 measured 4.5 to 7])
  pr []
  pr [Q1 what a long line costs]
  pr (se [  200 step edge] :lng [ms each])
  pr (se [  17 step edge] :shrt [ms each, P11 M0 measured 0.060 for fd 17])
  pr [  if these are close, section 10 is right and length is nearly free]
  pr []
  pr [Q2 what the present costs]
  pr (se [  splitscreen] :pres.split [ms, section 6 predicts 19.7])
  pr (se [  fullscreen ] :pres.full [ms, P11 M0 measured 26.3])
  pr (se [  the split saving is] :pres.full - :pres.split [ms])
  pr []
  pr [Q4 and Q5 what the objects cost to project]
  pr (se [  one box] :boxp [ms, the design predicts 2.16])
  pr (se [  the enemy] :enp [ms, the design predicts 6.0])
  pr (se [  one box drawn, 12 edges in 20 statements] :boxd [ms])
  pr (se [  the horizon, 32 points scanned] :hzn [ms, the design predicts 3.9 culled])
  pr (se [  of 32 points,] item 4 :p13m0.seen [were on screen])
  pr [  the design assumed about 12 of them, which is the number to check]
  pr []
  pr [the series]
  line.for 1
  line.for 2
  line.for 3
  line.for 4
  pr []
  pr [what the design predicts, from docs/battlezone-design.md section 12]
  pr [  51.2 ms total at 3 obstacles and 1 enemy, against 66.7 at 15 fps]
  pr [  present 19.7, horizon 3.9, cull 3.3, boxes 6.5, enemy 6.0, edges 4.0]
  pr []
  pr [the gate, from section 16]
  pr [  under 66.7 ms  build the game at L0]
  pr [  66.7 to 71.4   spend the two gameplay levers in section 14]
  pr [  over 71.4      stop and take L4, the drawmodel family]
  pr []
  pr (se [nodes at start] :n0 [at end] :n1 [after recycle] :n2)
  pr (se [word bytes free] :w0 [at start,] :w1 [at end,] :w2 [after recycle])
  pr []
end

to p13m0
  local "n0 local "n1 local "n2 local "w0 local "w1 local "w2
  local "arith local "bare local "lng local "shrt
  local "pres.split local "pres.full local "boxp local "enp
  local "boxd local "hzn
  recycle
  make "n0 nodes  make "w0 atoms

  set.mhz :p13m0.mhz
  make "p13m0.mhz.ran read.mhz
  make "p13m0.temp0 read.temp

  splitscreen
  window
  setrefresh "manual
  clean

  make "arith time.arith
  make "bare time.bare
  make "lng time.long.line
  make "shrt time.short.line
  make "boxp time.box.proj
  make "enp time.enemy.proj
  make "boxd time.box.draw
  make "hzn time.horizon

  make "pres.split time.present
  fullscreen
  clean
  make "pres.full time.present
  splitscreen
  clean

  measure 1
  measure 2
  measure 3
  measure 4

  make "p13m0.temp1 read.temp
  make "n1 nodes  make "w1 atoms
  recycle
  ; Read the recovered figure HERE, not inside the report: printing builds a
  ; `sentence` per line, so a `nodes` evaluated down there reads the report's
  ; own garbage back as if the recycle had under-recovered.  P10's script lost
  ; its first hardware run to exactly that.
  make "n2 nodes  make "w2 atoms

  setrefresh "auto
  textscreen
  open "p13m0.txt
  setwrite "p13m0.txt
  p13m0.report
  setwrite []
  close "p13m0.txt
  p13m0.report
  pr [Also written to p13m0.txt]
end
